No. `UpdateCostMetrics` has drifted from its design on one point: how it decides to seed the smoothed values.

**Intended behaviour.** The design doc is `docs/design-docs/design_docs/20261019-look-aside-balancer-fix-design.md`. It is the "Fixing Sub-Millisecond Herding (B + C)" note, in its "C: EWMA smoothing of cost metrics" section. The file is **missing** from the repo, so the only surviving copy of that text is in the sloppy note. It says to seed the smoothed values with the fresh sample directly when the previous sample has expired or never existed. The code in the note is:

```go
if nowTs-metrics.ts.Load() > b.metricExpireInterval {
    // seed with the fresh sample
} else {
    // apply EWMA
}
```

**Actual behaviour** (`refined_look_aside_balancer.go:258`):

```go
if math.Float64frombits(metrics.serviceTimeSmooth.Load()) == 0 {
    // seed with the fresh sample
} else {
    // apply EWMA
}
```

The code seeds whenever the smoothed service time is 0. It does not look at `ts` or `metricExpireInterval`. The note rejects this rule by name. Zero is ServiceTime's normal value for sub-millisecond requests, so it can't mean "unseeded". The differences are:

1. **Undamped outlier.** After a run of 0ms service-time samples, the smoothed value stays at 0. A single 20ms outlier then replaces it outright and becomes 20, where the EWMA would give 0.2×20 = 4. Smoothing exists to stop this kind of outlier from mis-triggering steering.
2. **Repeated re-seeding.** Any time the smoothed service time is 0, the next sample overwrites it. The same happens if a long run of 0s underflows the float back to 0. Only `serviceTimeSmooth` is checked, but both fields are overwritten together. A fresh `responseTime` therefore also replaces its smoothed value, even though the response-time history is non-zero.
3. **Stale values are not reset.** A node idle for longer than `metricExpireInterval` is meant to re-seed from its first fresh sample. The code instead blends that sample into the old stale value, unless that value happens to be exactly 0.

**Fix.** Replace the condition with `nowTs-metrics.ts.Load() > b.metricExpireInterval`. A brand-new entry has `ts == 0`, so it also takes the seed path. The comment above it (lines 256–257) also needs updating, because it describes the zero-value rule.

I haven't run the tests or checked whether `refined_look_aside_balancer_test.go` covers this case.

